---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getSummary } from '../lib/data';

const summary = getSummary();
---
<Layout title="About">
  <h1>About Metawatch</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Metawatch is a project of the <a href="https://iptc.org/">IPTC</a>. It periodically scans
    a curated list of major news publishers worldwide, samples the images they publish, and
    analyses each one for embedded metadata: EXIF, IPTC&nbsp;IIM, IPTC&nbsp;XMP, and C2PA.
  </p>

  <h2>Why?</h2>
  <p>
    Photographs carry information in their files: who took them, when, where, the licensing
    terms, captions and credits. The IPTC photo metadata standards exist so that this information
    travels with the image. In practice much of it is stripped — sometimes by publishers, sometimes
    by content delivery networks, sometimes by image processing pipelines. Metawatch surfaces what
    is happening at the surface of the open web.
  </p>

  <h2>How the score works</h2>
  <p>
    For each image we score 11 weighted fields drawn from IPTC&nbsp;IIM and IPTC&nbsp;XMP. A field
    counts as present if it appears in either family. The site score is the mean across that
    site's sampled images; the country score is the mean across that country's sites.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fields are: Creator (15), CopyrightNotice (15), CaptionAbstract (15), CreditLine (10),
    DateCreated (10), LocationCreated (10), Source (5), ObjectName (5), Keywords (5),
    WebStatement (5), LicensorURL (5). Weights total 100.
  </p>

  <h2>Sampling</h2>
  <p>
    For each site we read <code>robots.txt</code> and follow the sitemap chain it advertises,
    preferring news or picture sitemaps. We sample up to 20 articles from the last 30 days and
    take the top images from each. We respect <code>Crawl-delay</code> and rate-limit to one
    request per second per domain. Sites whose <code>robots.txt</code> disallows our user-agent
    (<code>IPTCMetadataCrawler</code>) are recorded but not crawled.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequency</h2>
  <p>
    Metawatch runs once per month, on the first of the month. Every run is committed to the
    project repository as a directory of Parquet files; the latest is exported to JSON and
    rendered into this static site.
  </p>

  <h2>Data</h2>
  <p>
    The full raw dataset (Parquet) is available in the repository under
    <code>data/runs/</code> and is licensed
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC&nbsp;BY 4.0</a>. The crawler source
    code is MIT-licensed and lives on
    <a href="https://github.com/iptc/metawatch">GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>For publishers</h2>
  <p>
    If you'd like to be removed from the crawled list, or to suggest improvements to your site's
    entry (a better sitemap URL, for example), please email
    <a href="mailto:office@iptc.org">office@iptc.org</a>. We honour removal requests without
    question.
  </p>

  <h2>Methodology limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We sample, we don't crawl exhaustively. A 20-article window may be unrepresentative for
        large publications.</li>
    <li>Field-level scoring rewards <em>presence</em>, not <em>correctness</em>. A photo with a
        valid <code>Creator</code> field of <code>"AP"</code> scores the same as one naming the
        actual photographer.</li>
    <li>"Stripped by CDN" vs "never embedded" is hard to distinguish from a single fetch — we
        plan to address this in Phase&nbsp;2.</li>
  </ul>

  {summary.run_id && (
    <p style="color:var(--iptc-muted);font-size:0.9rem">
      Latest run: <code>{summary.run_id}</code> — {summary.site_count_succeeded} sites,
      {summary.image_count} images.
    </p>
  )}
</Layout>
